<section class="mt-answer" aria-labelledby="quick-answer"><p class="eyebrow">The answer in 60 seconds</p><h2 id="quick-answer">Does MT7612U USB Wi-Fi work on Linux?</h2><p><strong>Normally, yes.</strong> MediaTek MT7612U is a dual-band Wi-Fi 5 USB chipset supported by Linux through the in-kernel <code>mt76x2u</code> driver. You generally do not need to download another driver. Missing Wi-Fi can instead mean missing firmware, a blocked radio or an unsuccessful router connection.</p><ol><li><code>lsusb</code>: Is the adapter visible, for example as <code>0e8d:7612</code>?</li><li><code>lsusb -t</code>: Does its USB interface show <code>Driver=mt76x2u</code>?</li><li><code>iw dev</code>: Has Linux registered a wireless interface? If not, check firmware and kernel logs.</li></ol><p class="mt-method">Reviewed on 19 November 2026. A source-based practical guide by Dennis Hilk, not a hands-on test of this particular adapter. Every sample output below is illustrative.</p></section>
